WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.987
Let us connect the major ideas from Module 5.

00:00:02.987 --> 00:00:07.117
AND, OR, and NOT allow us to build compound rules.

00:00:07.117 --> 00:00:12.602
When AND and OR appear together, use parentheses so the grouping is obvious.

00:00:12.602 --> 00:00:17.937
SQL uses three-valued logic: TRUE, FALSE, and UNKNOWN.

00:00:17.937 --> 00:00:19.942
WHERE keeps only TRUE.

00:00:19.942 --> 00:00:26.237
This is why NULL can cause rows to disappear even when a condition is not definitely false.

00:00:26.237 --> 00:00:30.867
NULL is not the same as zero, false, or blank text.

00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:33.467
Use IS NULL for missingness.

00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:40.812
Use Boolean predicates such as IS TRUE, IS FALSE, and IS UNKNOWN when nullable Boolean fields carry distinct

00:00:40.812 --> 00:00:47.292
meanings. Use IS DISTINCT FROM when you need a definite comparison involving NULL.

00:00:47.292 --> 00:00:53.187
NULLIF is useful for normalizing values such as blank text or zero denominators.

00:00:53.187 --> 00:00:57.672
COALESCE is useful when a fallback is meaningful and documented.

00:00:57.672 --> 00:01:04.057
CASE lets us classify rows, but branch order matters because the first true branch wins.

00:01:04.057 --> 00:01:07.487
Most importantly, validate edge cases.

00:01:07.487 --> 00:01:15.012
Test NULL, zero, blank text, whitespace, exact thresholds, explicit FALSE, and overlapping rules.

00:01:15.012 --> 00:01:16.957
If you keep one habit from this module, make it this:

00:01:16.957 --> 00:01:23.202
write the rule in plain language before you write the SQL.

00:01:23.202 --> 00:01:28.702
Then make the SQL reflect that rule as clearly as possible.

00:01:28.702 --> 00:01:32.232
That habit will help you in analytics, reporting, data engineering,

00:01:32.232 --> 00:01:38.163
and any project where database logic is making decisions about real records.
